{"records":[{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7msw2d62r6","title":"Stepwise activities of mSWI/SNF family chromatin remodeling complexes direct T cell activation and exhaustion ","description":"Unprocessed immunoblots for Battistello et al., Mol Cell 2023","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-16T18:00:12.634Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7msw2d62r6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7msw2d62r6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Cigall","last_name":"Kadoch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Iannis","last_name":"Aifantis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j8jrrn4t9k","title":"Anti-SLC7A11 monoclonal antibody Inhibits Tumor Progression through a Janus-faced Effect on CD4+ and CD8+ T Cells in Colorectal Cancer","description":"  While chimeric antigen receptor T cell therapy has demonstrated significant efficacy in treating hematological malignancies, its application in solid tumors remains challenging. A primary factor contributing to the limited efficacy of CAR-T cells in solid tumors is the dysfunction of CD8+ T cells, which is often a result of exhaustion. However, emerging evidence suggests that modulating cellular metabolism holds promise for enhancing the functionality of CD8+ T cells. An experimental model of CD8⁺ T cell exhaustion was established, followed by RNA sequencing to profile the expression of SLC family genes, which are implicated in metabolic regulation and CD8⁺ T cell differentiation. Monoclonal antibodies were generated using hybridoma technology and subsequently humanized. The effects of antibody treatment on CD8⁺ T cell differentiation and exhaustion were assessed via flow cytometry and western blotting. To evaluate therapeutic efficacy, cell-derived xenograft models of colorectal and breast cancer were established, and the combined effect of the antibody with CAR-T cells was examined. Furthermore, CRISPR/Cas9-mediated gene-edited mouse models were generated, and single-cell sequencing was performed to monitor changes in tumor burden and the differentiation status of tumor-infiltrating CD8⁺ T cells. RNA sequencing analysis revealed that SLC7A11 was significantly upregulated in exhausted CD8⁺ T cells. A monoclonal antibody specifically targeting SLC7A11 was generated, and its binding affinity was rigorously validated. Functional studies demonstrated that inhibition of SLC7A11 promoted the expansion of CD8⁺ T stem cell memory cells and attenuated the exhausted phenotype. In vivo studies further showed that anti-SLC7A11 treatment enhanced the antitumor efficacy of CAR-T cells. The effects were mediated through the suppression of cystine uptake via SLC7A11 inhibition, thereby modulating the GCN2-eIF2α-SLC1A5 signaling axis. Restricting cystine uptake enhances the efficacy of CD8+ CAR-T cells and alleviates tumor burden.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-02T19:45:17.43Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j8jrrn4t9k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j8jrrn4t9k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haopeng","last_name":"Hong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["High-Throughput Sequencing","Single-Cell RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kdxhf7pmmk","title":"Gene Regulatory Programs that Specify Age-Related Differences during Thymocyte Development ","description":"T cell development is fundamental to immune system establishment, yet how this development changes with age remains poorly understood. Here, we construct a transcriptional and epigenetic atlas of T cell developmental programs in neonatal and adult mice, revealing the ontogeny of divergent gene regulatory programs and their link to age-related differences in phenotype and function. Specifically, we identify a gene module that diverges with age from the earliest stages of genesis and includes programs that govern effector response and cell cycle regulation. Moreover, we reveal that neonates possess more accessible chromatin during early thymocyte development, likely establishing poised gene expression programs that manifest later in thymocyte development. Finally, we leverage this atlas, employing a CRISPR-based perturbation approach coupled with single-cell RNA sequencing as a readout to uncover a conserved transcriptional regulator, Zbtb20, that contributes to age-dependent differences in T cell development. Altogether, our study defines transcriptional and epigenetic programs that regulate age-specific differences in T cell development (All genomic data contained in this manuscript has been deposited at GEO (GSE267922, GSE267918 and GSE267923)).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-03T15:26:38.719Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kdxhf7pmmk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kdxhf7pmmk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Divya","last_name":"Ganapathi Sankaran","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Matrix"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f52y9zkpzm","title":"RNF31 inhibition sensitizes tumors to bystander killing by innate and adaptive immune cells","description":"Tumor escape mechanisms for immunotherapy include deficiencies in antigen presentation, diminishing adaptive CD8+ T cell antitumor activity. Although innate NK cells are triggered by loss of MHC class I, their response is often inadequate. To increase tumor susceptibility to both innate and adaptive immune elimination, we performed parallel genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens under NK and CD8+ T cell pressure. We identified all components, RNF31, RBCK1, and SHARPIN, of the linear ubiquitination chain assembly complex (LUBAC). Genetic and pharmacologic ablation of RNF31, an E3 ubiquitin ligase, strongly sensitized melanoma, breast and colorectal cancer cells to both NK and CD8+ T cell killing. This occurred in a TNF-dependent manner, causing loss of A20 and non-canonical IKK complexes from TNF Receptor Complex I. Corroborating this preclinically, a small molecule RNF31 inhibitor sensitized human colon carcinoma organoids to TNF and greatly enhanced immune bystander killing of antigen-loss and antigen presentation machinery-deficient tumor cells. These results merit exploration of RNF31 inhibition as a clinical pharmacological opportunity for immunotherapy-refractory cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-30T07:01:54.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f52y9zkpzm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f52y9zkpzm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengkui","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c297bm6k96","title":"Transcriptomic Analysis of T cell Exhaustion Signatures Reveals a Severity Spectrum in Chronic Skin Diseases","description":"Transcriptomic analysis was performed on publicly available skin biopsy datasets of melanoma, plaque psoriasis, rosacea, cutaneous lupus erythematosus, leprosy, leishmaniasis, and diabetic foot ulcer patients. Immune cell proportions were estimated using CIBERSORTx with the LM22 signature matrix.  A panel of 42 T cell exhaustion–related genes was extracted to construct a matrix for Principal Component Analysis (PCA), enabling comparison of exhaustion signatures. \n T cell infiltration and transcriptomic profiling revealed three core exhaustion patterns: γδ T cell expansion, CD4⁺ memory imbalance, and Treg depletion. PCA stratified diseases by exhaustion severity, with melanoma, psoriasis, and leishmaniasis showing terminal exhaustion signatures, while rosacea and lupus subtypes exhibited intermediate profiles.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-23T18:54:27.931Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c297bm6k96","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c297bm6k96"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Luisa","last_name":"Ruiz","orcid":"0000-0001-6515-6063"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Dermatology","Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":124033,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":117,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":124033,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":123560,"restricted":61,"embargoed":412},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2062},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1348},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":597},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":445},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":383},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":342},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":280},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":258}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":121455},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1893},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":178},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","c